meta {
  name: Set user status
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/users/{{userId}}/status
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "isActive": false
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  # Set user status

  `PATCH /api/users/{{userId}}/status`

  Assign the supplied active status to a user.

  ## Authentication

  Admin; inherited Bearer token.

  ## Parameters

  | Name | Location | Meaning |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | userId | Path | GUID; replace the example with a real resource. |

  ## Request body

  | Field | Type | Rules / default |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | isActive | boolean | false if omitted; true activates, false deactivates. |

  Send Content-Type: application/json.

  ```json
  {
    "isActive": false
  }
  ```

  ## Response

  HTTP **200**.

  ```json
  {
    "message": "User status successfully updated to Inactive."
  }
  ```

  ## Errors

  - 400: model validation.
  - 404: User not found.
  - 401: missing, invalid, or expired Bearer token.
  - 403: authenticated token with an insufficient role.

  Automatic model validation can return ValidationProblemDetails; explicit controller errors use a message field. Authentication middleware errors may have no body.

  ## Notes

  Sets the value rather than toggling it. Existing JWTs are not revoked. No guard prevents deactivating the caller or last administrator.

  Examples contain fictitious data. The assertion checks the successful scenario; a missing record or insufficient permissions can produce a different documented status.
}
